# nomo snapshot v1
# worksheet: plots

=== text ===
# Plots
`plot(f, a, b)` samples a function across a span and draws it. Like `map`,
`iterate`, `root` and `integral`, it takes the **name** of a function — there
are no lambdas, and a call's callee is always a name.
fn parabola defined
plot(parabola, 0 - 3, 3) = [plot parabola: -3 to 3, 257 points, -2 to 7]
A fixed number of samples, whatever the span. That is `integral`'s rule — a
fixed amount of work rather than a tolerance test — and it is what makes a
drawing terminate and come out the same on every machine.
The span carries units, and so does what the function returns. Both axes are
labelled from the dimensions rather than from anything the author writes
twice.
w = 12 kN/m
L = 6 m
fn moment defined
plot(moment, 0 m, L) = plot(moment, 0 m, 6 m) = [plot moment: 0 to 6 m, 257 points, 0 to 54000 J]
A curve that leaves the plane is drawn with a gap rather than a line through
values the function never took. The summary line counts them.
fn reciprocal defined
plot(reciprocal, 0 - 1, 1) = [plot reciprocal: -1 to 1, 257 points, -128 to 128, 1 gap(s)]
The gain of an LLC resonant tank against switching frequency — the curve
`examples/llc.nomo` designs against, and the reason a worksheet language
needs plots at all. Its peak is the thing an engineer is looking for.
f0 = 110000 Hz
Ln = 3.7
Qe = 0.41
fn gain defined
plot(gain, 30 kHz, 200 kHz) = [plot gain: 30000 to 200000 Hz, 257 points, 0.25853 to 1.86276]
A plot is a value, so it can be bound to a name. It is not a number, though:
there is no arithmetic on one.
picture = plot(parabola, 0, 1) = [plot parabola: 0 to 1, 257 points, -2 to -1]
A flat function still has a chart: the axis is given an extent rather than
dividing by one of zero width.
fn level defined
plot(level, 0, 1) = [plot level: 0 to 1, 257 points, 5 to 5 m]
Several curves on one plot: every argument but the last two names one. They
share the span and the vertical axis, so a gain drawn beside a length is
refused rather than given two meanings on one axis.
fn gain_at defined
A family of curves is written by naming each member. That is what a language
with no lambdas has instead of one, and it is what an engineer wants anyway:
the load the tank is designed at has a name on the page.
fn light defined
fn nominal defined
fn heavy defined
plot(light, nominal, heavy, 30 kHz, 200 kHz) = [plot light, nominal, heavy: 30000 to 200000 Hz, 257 points, 0.221418 to 3.61539]
A table of measured points is the other kind of plot: an n-by-2 matrix, x in
the first column and y in the second. No span is written and none is needed —
the points brought their own x — so the horizontal axis is fitted to the data
the way the vertical one always was.
deflection = [[0 kN, 0 mm], [10 kN, 1.9 mm], [20 kN, 4.1 mm], [30 kN, 6.2 mm], [40 kN, 8.5 mm], [50 kN, 11.4 mm]] = [[0 N, 0 m], [10000 N, 0.0019 m], [20000 N, 0.0041 m], [30000 N, 0.0062 m], [40000 N, 0.0085 m], [50000 N, 0.0114 m]]
plot(deflection) = plot([[0 N, 0 m], [10000 N, 0.0019 m], [20000 N, 0.0041 m], [30000 N, 0.0062 m], [40000 N, 0.0085 m], [50000 N, 0.0114 m]]) = [plot deflection: 0 to 50000 N, 6 points, 0 to 0.0114 m]
The shape `augment` builds, which is how a worksheet plots something it just
computed: two columns joined into a table of points.
t = range(0, 8) = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
fn drop defined
fallen = augment(t s, map(drop, t)) = augment([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8] s, map(drop, [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8])) = [[0 s, 0 m], [1 s, 2.45 m], [2 s, 9.8 m], [3 s, 22.05 m], [4 s, 39.2 m], [5 s, 61.25 m], [6 s, 88.2 m], [7 s, 120.05 m], [8 s, 156.8 m]]
plot(fallen) = plot([[0 s, 0 m], [1 s, 2.45 m], [2 s, 9.8 m], [3 s, 22.05 m], [4 s, 39.2 m], [5 s, 61.25 m], [6 s, 88.2 m], [7 s, 120.05 m], [8 s, 156.8 m]]) = [plot fallen: 0 to 8 s, 9 points, 0 to 156.8 m]
Several tables on one plot, and each carries the name it was written under.
model = [[0 kN, 0 mm], [50 kN, 11 mm]] = [[0 N, 0 m], [50000 N, 0.011 m]]
plot(deflection, model) = plot([[0 N, 0 m], [10000 N, 0.0019 m], [20000 N, 0.0041 m], [30000 N, 0.0062 m], [40000 N, 0.0085 m], [50000 N, 0.0114 m]], [[0 N, 0 m], [50000 N, 0.011 m]]) = [plot deflection, model: 0 to 50000 N, 6, 2 points, 0 to 0.0114 m]

